														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4834758">
									

<p>ok. I am willing to <strong><span style="color: green">GREENLIGHT</span></strong> this, but I am going to warn you that this doesn't seem to be a strong narrative.</p>
<p>A few nitpicks:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">This</span>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This is part of the reason I intend to add the audio file, not just for the story/narrative, but for the sound effects the skip makes, (cracking, sloshing, banging, skittering, etc.) and the scientist's reactions, which I think will add to the discomfort factor (I know audio is what made SCP-682 so infamous, not that my intention is to mimic that article).</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>Does not address my concern below:</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>so just a gory description of this anomaly isn’t really story rich, or all that unique</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Body horror is very common on the wiki (suggest you look at Sarkicism Hub) so even if the audio is gross and the description is gross&#8230; that's not a story. For example, have you seen any slasher horror movies? Impressive gory violence isn't exactly a story beat&#8230; its meant to <strong>shock</strong></p>
<p>What I am saying is: <em>DONT rely only on the body horror to make your article work. Do more than imply characterization, because without something to relate to&#8230; the reader will just think &quot;gross&quot; and maybe that'll work for some but just as likely it won't work for others.</em></p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>The second addendum mentions that other researchers heard the whisper, describing it as an indiscriminate noise, and recommended further research in case it meant something (without mentioning what the whisper said, or that it was even a whisper).</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>If I am not mistaken, we (as the readers) will hear this whisper in the audiofile, yes? So if we can understand it, how would 2 separate researchers be unable to recognize speech?</em></p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>The origin is implied in the description section. I mention the shrunken portions of the brain that control cognition, motor functions, memory, etc, without explicitly stating their function- it just says frontal lobe, parietal lobe, and hippocampus without explaining to the reader why that's significant; I don't intend to talk down to them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>To me, this would not imply that the fetus took the mother's brain. A fetus has a brain I think&#8230; or a protobrain like organ that is developing.</em></p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>Essentially it's a downer ending as the skip can't get what it wants, despite its hardships. Because its still technically alive at the end, it's left open-ended, though. Do you think it should not be open-ended?</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>I think &quot;open ended&quot; can be good. This does not seem open-ended, it seems shallow of facts/plot. I think you need to expand this in terms of the narrative&#8230; why would the child do this? Why would this have happened at all? Why should the Foundation care at all?</em></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

