														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4834743">
									

<p>PM request acknowledged, time for a followup! :)</p>
<p><strong>SAND</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: An Indian shield from the 16th century that has connection to the 3 main Hindu gods. When held by a living person with the belief of doing heroic deeds will cause a blue energy field to cover it and tempt them to do said heroic deeds. The energy field can redirect any damage that is inflicted upon it right back at the attacker (Examples: Shooting it with a gun will result in bullet wounds appearing in you or slashing at it with a sharp weapon will give you stab wounds and cuts). If its wielder has villainous or wrongful ideas then the shield will be corrupted and its energy field will turn red, fueling its wielder's ideas.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Overall, I don't have much of an issue with the concept, you seem to have a strong grasp on what the skip is. My only main issue with the concept is the the skips overall purpose, as it doesn't seem to be expanded upon much. which in turn makes the concept feel a bit flat.</p>
<p>The current focus: &quot;if you're good then the shield will be blue, and you do good things. And if you're bad the shield will be red, and you do bad things&quot;, is a bit of cliche as it feels like it's just making the wearer a super hero or super villain without bringing anything new in.</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: The shield came to the foundation's attention when reports of a man wielding a glowing blue shield was committing vigilantism in India. An investigation was initiated as several agents were sent to recover it. They were able to subdue the vigilante, but once they removed the shield his body crumbled to dust only leaving behind his equipment and some notes. In the process of being taken to the Foundation it ended up causing some trouble along the way as it switched between blue and red when held by different personnel, causing suspicion and conflict among them. Once finally contained, tests are conducted on the shield and the notes are analyzed to learn more about it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I quiet liked where the story was going with the suspicion between personnel, but that avenue just sort of stopped abruptly in favour of testing the skip, which made the payoff feel anticlimactic.- what came of the suspicions? who were these characters? what interventions did they have? whats everything building up to?</p>
<p>Along with this, I feel the setup could be stronger as currently the original wearer just sort of feels like a plot device for the Foundation, as he doesn't really contribute much to the narrative, or give any sort of insight into the skip. Rather, he just disappears with no context or development, which feels like a quick way to get out of writing character interactions, especially since the &quot;crumbling into dust&quot; isn't mentioned before or after this.- why does this happen? does the Foundation ever get an any context to this? do they research it? why isn't it mentioned again? what did the &quot;vigilante&quot; actually do?</p>
<p><strong>Red or green?</strong><br />
Due to my afore mentioned concerns, no-greenlight</p>
<p>-I would recommend focusing your narrative more around the suspicions between characters, and the shields way of showing &quot;good&quot; and &quot;bad&quot;.</p>
<p>Also, try to keep away from just turning the wearer's into super heroes (or villains) as it makes the concept feel cliched and hard to get invested in, as characters are much more complex than just &quot;good&quot; or &quot;bad&quot;, what where the characters intentions? who were they? why should I as reader care about them? what is considered &quot;good&quot; and &quot;bad&quot; ect.</p>
<p>If you would like any additional feedback, feel welcome to send me a PM :) I look forward to any further edits! and if you're looking for any new perspectives or other critters in general, I would highly recommend the butterfly squad.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>RED</p>

