														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4834727">
									

<p>Elevator Pitch: An antique toybox of considerable age may be far more dangerous than it initially appears.</p>
<p>Summary: While I've written up more extensive details, I understand this is just the pitch, so, here we go. I don't -think- this idea has been used yet, though with 4,000+ articles, I could very much be wrong.</p>
<p>The item is an antique toybox, high quality wood with pig iron fittings, with an integral locking function. One, and only one, key has been found to work. Can be freely opened and closed when not locked. When unlocked, may only be locked by an adult, with a few additional requirements. When unlocked, may only be unlocked by a &quot;child&quot; (12 or younger), or when such an individual is present in the room.</p>
<p>The contents vary somewhat, but always contain a selection of toys, which may be freely removed by anyone once the box is unlocked. They consist of examples from a broad swathe of history, but have one thing in common: all are toys that are notoriously dangerous to children, many of which have been recalled. All are examples that have occurred within our own reality. Ie:</p>
<ul>
<li>The notorious Battlestar Galactica toy whose missile firing mechanism caused a child to choke to death.</li>
<li>The Barbie doll whose sparking rollerblades caused fires.</li>
<li>A &quot;lazertag&quot; set circa 1985 that makes it appear that the players are using real guns, presenting an alarming scene to law enforcers. (this resulted in shooting deaths in the 1980s)</li>
<li>A set of large plastic &quot;conkers&quot; attached to elastic strings that can damage a child when used enthusiastically.</li>
<li>A bag of marbles that look very much like candy</li>
<li>A children's &quot;Russian Roulette&quot; Game</li>
<li>A Teddy Ruxpin whose tapes, when played backward, give terrible advice to children (&quot;Let's play with Daddy's Guns!&quot; &quot;Let's open Mommy's Liquor Cabinet&quot;)</li>
<li>A cape from a prominent superhero franchise (historically based upon the 1960s Batman television series) that makes the wearer feel as if they can glide to earth from garage roofs, the tops of buildings, etc.</li>
</ul>
<p>I imagine this would be a Euclid class object, requiring minimal maintenance but careful observation. Occasionally, new toys will appear, old toys will disappear, but those will invariably show up again depending upon the individual in question.</p>
<p>Thanks for your time!</p>
<p>-Alex</p>

